Z||000001||Old Lar||07-06-2000||05:54 AM||||The convertible looks nice, but how was it constructed?  I saw one Indy Pace car that was made into a convertible and the frame was not reinforced and it was sagging in the middle.<P>Photos don't show the real truth.<P>Why don't they post the reserve on these cars?  So you won't waste your time.||||reg||4
Z||000002||87ECO5||07-06-2000||07:12 AM||||The ad says the top folds down behind the seats.  How is that possible with the ECM there?<P>BTW, I've heard the X-frames they use to strengthen the bodies on these things is welded into place - OVER THE FUEL TANK!  Anyone know if someone's designed a "bolt-on" version?  Those fuel pumps don't last forever, ya know!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 07-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-06-2000||08:38 AM||||The X-frames for the convertables is an old way to do it. They did that back when Fiero's were still being made. The best way to do it is pound square stock thru the frame rails and attach to the subframes on each side.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||07-06-2000||10:53 AM||||You can see in the picture that toe door is out of whack.||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||07-06-2000||03:42 PM||||Hi Oreif. Could you describe the frame strengthening technique a bit more? This should probably be a new topic in the tech section but anyways.<P>I'm not sure what you mean by "attach to the subframes on each side". What advantages are there over the use of a bottom x-frame? I'm very interested in learning about the differences. In fact I've been toying with the idea of making my Testarossa rebody project car a topless roadster. If you have further info on the subject please reply (perhaps in the tech section). Thanks!!!<BR>||||reg||1
